        [PexMethod]
        public void PUT_RemoveAtContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int index)
        {
            if (0 <= index && index < arrList.Count)
            {
                AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

                int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
                int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf((int)arrList[index]);
                int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf((int)arrList[index]);
                bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains((int)arrList[index]);

                int x = (int) arrList[index];
                arrList.RemoveAt(index);

                int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
                int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
                int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
                bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
                PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);


                // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  @@@ new array list has size at most as much as old array list 
                // The idea is if the element removed was contained in the array list, it should be taken out of it. 
        Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount, "The new array list has size greater than the old array list.");
      }

        }
    }
}

The code is for generating test coverage for the ArrayList class. This code is an example for a pairwise test case. The other test cases (like Add1, Add2, Add3 etc.) are generated similarly. The PUT_AddContract and PUT_RemoveContract method generates two test cases for Add and Remove operations. 

The PUT_RemoveAtContract generates a test case for RemoveAt operation. The PexObserve.ValueForViewing is used to obtain the values of the variables involved so that they can be viewed in the Pex Studio’s test result grid. The assert is used to ensure that the size property of the new array list is not greater than old array list. The code was run with multiple inputs generated by Pex as a pairwise test coverage. After the test run, the results were examined and the coverage was analyzed.

Anyway please correct me if my analysis is incorrect. Thanks.

A: Your analysis is largely correct. You've understood the purpose of the PexObserve.ValueForViewing method, which is essentially to provide a visual interface for viewing the test case parameters and their corresponding outcomes. You've also grasped the basic principles behind Pairwise Method, which is a technique for generating tests from a set of method's input combinations in order to increase code coverage.

In the case you described, you used the PexObserve.ValueForViewing to illustrate the inputs and outputs of your PUT_RemoveAtContract method. This gives you a nice visual representation of how the method behaves, which can be very useful in understanding what'--------------------	    // that assert new_arrListCount <= old_arrListCount + 1
	    // since after a remove, arraylist has one less element.
	    Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount <= Old_arrListCount + 1); 

        }

	    // other possible contracts could be: 
	    // - size of resulting list after a sort operation
	    // - position where an element would have been inserted by a 'BinarySearch' method (position or -1 if couldn't insert)
	    // - number of duplicates after an 'Add' operation (only if it resulted in a change)
        // - capacity after a 'TrimToSize' or 'TrimExcess' operation
    }
