The above code assumes that we have an `ArrayList` object `arrList` and an integer object `x`. It adds an assumption that tests should only be run against the `ArrayList` under test.  

The `PUT_AddContract` and `PUT_RemoveContract` methods test the `Add` and `Remove` methods of the `ArrayList`, respectively, and verify that the correct post-conditions are maintained following each operation.

The `PexObserve.ValueForViewing` lines are used to record the changed values for viewing in the interactive mode of Pex. 

For `PUT_RemoveContract`, an assertion is added that new ArrayList has size at most as old ArrayList, proving that the test coverage is not only increased but also optimized to reduce the potential test failures.


It's easy to add more contracts here, such as contracts for Binary search, sorting, to String conversion and so on. You can use the similar approach to test all other methods in your class. 

**Test all methods with Pex** 
You can also use Pex to test all the methods of your application. For each method in the production code, create a Pex method decorated with `[PexMethod]` and call the method with as many arguments as it has parameters. You can add `AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true)` to all the methods. 

Remember Pex is a static code analyzer which tries to explore all possible execution paths during execution and might not be able to find out all possible bugs. Therefore, it's always a best practice to perform unit testing and other forms of checking of the code logic manually as well before fully relying on Pex.
 
*Further Reading:*
* [NUnit's ExpectedException attribute](https://github.com/nunit/docs/wiki/ExpectedException)
* [Assert Class](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.visualstudio.testtools.unittesting.assert?view=mstest-2.2.1)
* [Pex Class](https://rehansaeed.com/partial-method-testing-with-microsoft-pex-2-0/)
